window.__labLoaded({"id":"5.1","title":"Confidence Intervals","goal":"Build a 95% z-interval for a mean when sigma is known, then simulate thousands of intervals to see about 95% catch the true mean.","question":"A standardised exam is known to have an SD of 12 points (sigma = 12). A random sample of 40 students averaged 78.5. The question: what is the true average for ALL students? A single number hides the uncertainty, so we build a 95% confidence interval and then test whether such intervals really catch the truth.","python":{"blocks":[{"title":"The z-interval by formula","text":"The lesson's exam: sigma = 12 is known, n = 40 and the sample mean is 78.5. Three steps: standard error, margin of error (z* times SE), then the interval.","code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nsigma, n, xbar = 12, 40, 78.5\nz = stats.norm.ppf(0.975)\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nme = z * se\n\nprint(f'z* = {z:.3f}, SE = {se:.3f}, margin = {me:.2f}')\nprint(f'95% CI = ({xbar - me:.2f}, {xbar + me:.2f})')","explain":["Load NumPy for the square root and arrays.","Load SciPy's stats tools, which hold the normal distribution.","Store the three facts we know: sigma (known SD of scores), n (sample size) and xbar (sample mean).","Find z*, the number of standard errors that captures the middle 95%: ppf(0.975) leaves 2.5% in each tail.","Standard error of the mean: sigma divided by the square root of n.","Margin of error = z* times SE, the 'plus or minus' of the interval.","Print z*, the SE and the margin, rounded.","Print the interval: the estimate minus and plus the margin."],"meaning":"z* is 1.960, the standard error is 1.897 and the margin of error is 3.72 points. So the 95% interval is 74.78 to 82.22. We are 95% confident the true average for all students lies in that range, not that 95% of students score in it.","out":"z* = 1.960, SE = 1.897, margin = 3.72\n95% CI = (74.78, 82.22)","figs":[]},{"title":"Confidence level changes the width","text":"Being more confident means casting a wider net. We repeat the calculation for 90%, 95% and 99%, using the matching z*.","code":"for level in (0.90, 0.95, 0.99):\n    z = stats.norm.ppf(1 - (1 - level) / 2)\n    me = z * se\n    print(f'{level:.0%}: z* = {z:.3f}, margin = {me:.2f}, ({xbar - me:.2f}, {xbar + me:.2f})')","explain":["Loop over three confidence levels.","Find z* for each level; 1 - (1 - level)/2 puts the leftover probability equally in both tails.","Compute the margin of error at that level, reusing the same standard error.","Print the level, z*, the margin and the interval."],"meaning":"More confidence needs a wider net: 90% gives a margin of 3.12 (75.38 to 81.62), 95% gives 3.72 (74.78 to 82.22) and 99% gives 4.89 (73.61 to 83.39). The price of being surer is a vaguer answer.","out":"90%: z* = 1.645, margin = 3.12, (75.38, 81.62)\n95%: z* = 1.960, margin = 3.72, (74.78, 82.22)\n99%: z* = 2.576, margin = 4.89, (73.61, 83.39)","figs":[]},{"title":"Sample size changes the width","text":"At 95%, quadrupling n halves the margin of error, the 1 over square root of n rule again.","code":"for n_try in (10, 40, 160, 640):\n    margin = 1.96 * sigma / np.sqrt(n_try)\n    print(f'n = {n_try:3d}: margin = {margin:.2f}')","explain":["Loop over four sample sizes, each four times the last.","Margin of error at 95% for that n, using z* = 1.96.","Print n and the margin."],"meaning":"The margin falls from 7.44 at n = 10, to 3.72 at n = 40, 1.86 at n = 160 and 0.93 at n = 640. Each time n is quadrupled the margin halves, the 1 over square root of n rule again.","out":"n =  10: margin = 7.44\nn =  40: margin = 3.72\nn = 160: margin = 1.86\nn = 640: margin = 0.93","figs":[]},{"title":"Does it really catch the truth?","text":"Make the truth known: a normal population with mean 78.5 and SD 12. Draw 10,000 samples of 40, build a 95% interval from each, and count how many contain 78.5.","code":"rng = np.random.default_rng(1)\ntrue_mean = 78.5\nmeans = rng.normal(true_mean, sigma, size=(10_000, 40)).mean(axis=1)\nme = 1.96 * sigma / np.sqrt(40)\ncaught = (means - me <= true_mean) & (true_mean <= means + me)\n\nprint(f'intervals that caught the true mean: {caught.mean():.1%}')\nprint('intervals that missed:', (~caught).sum(), 'of 10000')","explain":["Make a seeded random generator so the check is repeatable.","Pretend we know the truth: the real mean is 78.5.","Draw 10,000 samples of 40 scores from a normal population (mean 78.5, SD 12) and take each sample's mean.","Margin of error for n = 40, as before.","For each sample mean, check whether the interval mean +/- margin contains the true mean.","Print the share of intervals that caught the true mean.","Print how many missed."],"meaning":"Of 10,000 intervals, 95.0% caught the true mean and 503 missed. That is exactly what '95% confident' means: the METHOD captures the truth about 95 times in 100, though any single interval either does or does not.","out":"intervals that caught the true mean: 95.0%\nintervals that missed: 503 of 10000","figs":[]}]},"r":{"code":"sigma <- 12; n <- 40; xbar <- 78.5\nz <- qnorm(0.975)\nme <- z * sigma / sqrt(n)\nc(z, me, xbar - me, xbar + me)\nset.seed(1)\nmeans <- replicate(10000, mean(rnorm(40, 78.5, 12)))\nmean(abs(means - 78.5) <= me)","expect":"z = 1.960, margin = 3.72, interval 74.78 to 82.22; coverage close to 0.95 (Python: 95.0%)","note":"Base R has no z-interval command, so we use qnorm and the formula. The coverage figure is simulated, so it is close to 95% but not identical to Python's.","explain":["Store sigma, n and the sample mean on one line using semicolons.","z* from the normal distribution: qnorm(0.975).","Margin of error = z* times sigma / sqrt(n).","Print z*, the margin and the interval limits in one vector.","Fix the random seed.","Simulate 10,000 sample means of 40 scores from a normal population (78.5, 12).","Print the share of sample means within one margin of the truth; this equals the share of intervals that catch it."],"read":"The c(...) line prints 1.960, 3.72, 74.78 and 82.22, matching Python. The last line should print close to 0.95: the coverage, simulated so close to but not identical to Python's 95.0%."},"spss":{"menu":"Transform > Compute Variable (use IDF.NORMAL in the function list)","syntax":"DATA LIST FREE / sigma n xbar.\nBEGIN DATA\n12 40 78.5\nEND DATA.\nCOMPUTE z = IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).\nCOMPUTE se = sigma / SQRT(n).\nCOMPUTE me = z * se.\nCOMPUTE lower = xbar - me.\nCOMPUTE upper = xbar + me.\nLIST VARIABLES=z se me lower upper.","note":"SPSS has no menu for a known-sigma interval, so we compute it from the formula; IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1) gives z* = 1.960. The coverage simulation is not repeated here.","explain":["Declare three variables to read from the data lines.","Marks the start of the typed-in data.","One row: sigma 12, n 40, mean 78.5.","Marks the end of the data.","z* from the normal distribution: IDF.NORMAL(0.975, 0, 1).","Standard error = sigma / sqrt(n).","Margin of error = z* times SE.","Lower limit = mean minus margin.","Upper limit = mean plus margin.","List the results so they appear in the output."],"read":"The listing shows z about 1.960, se 1.897, me 3.72, lower 74.78 and upper 82.22, the same as Python."},"stata":{"code":"scalar z = invnormal(0.975)\nscalar se = 12 / sqrt(40)\nscalar me = z * se\ndisplay \"z* = \" z\ndisplay \"SE = \" se\ndisplay \"margin = \" me\ndisplay \"95% CI = (\" (78.5 - me) \", \" (78.5 + me) \")\"","note":"Stata's own interval commands assume sigma is unknown (t-based), so the known-sigma interval is built from invnormal() and the formula. It matches Python's 74.78 to 82.22.","explain":["Store z*, found with invnormal(0.975), as a scalar called z.","Standard error 12 / sqrt(40) as a scalar.","Margin of error = z times se.","Print z* with a label.","Print the standard error.","Print the margin.","Print the interval limits, computed on the fly."],"read":"The five display lines show z* = 1.96, SE = 1.897, margin = 3.72 and the interval (74.78, 82.22). These match Python exactly because no randomness is involved."},"conclusion":"With 40 students averaging 78.5 and sigma = 12, the 95% interval for the true average is 74.78 to 82.22. The simulation shows the method works: 95.0% of intervals caught the truth. Caution: this recipe assumes sigma is truly known and the sample is random; if sigma is estimated from the data, use the t-interval of the next lesson.","practice":{"scenario":"A packing machine fills bags of rice. Its fill weight is known to vary with an SD of 0.5 kg (sigma = 0.5). An inspector weighs 64 random bags and finds an average of 12.3 kg. She wants an interval for the machine's true average fill and to know how large a sample would shrink the margin to 0.05 kg.","data":"sigma = 0.5, n = 64, sample mean = 12.3 kg.","tasks":["Build the 95% interval for the true mean fill.","Build the 99% interval and compare its width.","How many bags would give a 95% margin of 0.05 kg?"],"hint":"Use xbar +/- z* * sigma/sqrt(n). For the sample size, rearrange margin = 1.96 * sigma / sqrt(n) to n = (1.96 * sigma / margin)^2 and round up.","solution":{"code":"import numpy as np\nfrom scipy import stats\nsigma, n, xbar = 0.5, 64, 12.3\nse = sigma / np.sqrt(n)\nfor level in (0.95, 0.99):\n    z = stats.norm.ppf(1 - (1 - level) / 2)\n    print(f'{level:.0%}: z* = {z:.3f}, margin = {z * se:.3f}, ({xbar - z * se:.2f}, {xbar + z * se:.2f})')\nneed = int(np.ceil((1.96 * sigma / 0.05) ** 2))\nprint('n for a 95% margin of 0.05:', need)","answer":"The 95% interval is 12.18 to 12.42 kg (margin 0.122) and the 99% interval is 12.14 to 12.46 (margin 0.161). The 99% interval is wider: being surer costs precision. To cut the 95% margin to 0.05 kg she would need 385 bags.","out":"95%: z* = 1.960, margin = 0.122, (12.18, 12.42)\n99%: z* = 2.576, margin = 0.161, (12.14, 12.46)\nn for a 95% margin of 0.05: 385","figs":[]}}});
